name: interfaces and addresses
boss: Netcat
flavor: I listen where you cannot see.
brief: An interface is a way onto a network. ip is the modern tool for all of
brief: it -- addresses, links, routes, neighbours -- and ifconfig and route are
brief: the older ones you will still meet on machines that have not moved on.
brief:
brief: ip has a shape: ip OBJECT COMMAND. ip addr, ip link, ip route, ip neigh.
brief: Every one of them takes show, add and del.
---
q: %show% every interface and its addresses
a: ip addr
out: .
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: ip addr with no filter lists every interface and its addresses
---
q: %show% addresses one per line, in a form that is easy to cut up
a: ip -o addr
out: .
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: -o (oneline) puts each address on its own line for easy parsing
---
q: %show% only ipv4 addresses
a: ip -4 addr
out: .
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: -4 filters ip's output down to ipv4 only
---
q: %show% the links, without their addresses
a: ip link
out: .
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: ip link shows interfaces themselves, not the addresses on them
---
q: {bring the interface @iface@ up|bring @iface@ up|set @iface@ up}
a: ip link set @iface@ up
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: ip link set IFACE up/down toggles an interface's state
---
q: {bring @iface@ back down|bring @iface@ down|set @iface@ down}
a: ip link set @iface@ down
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: down is the mirror of up
---
q: {give @iface@ the address @ip@ with a 24 bit netmask|assign @iface@ the address @ip@ with a /24 netmask|add the address @ip@/24 to @iface@}
a: ip addr add @ip@/24 dev @iface@
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: addr add takes address/prefixlen, and dev to say where
---
q: {take that address off @iface@ again|remove that address from @iface@|delete the address you just added from @iface@}
a: ip addr del @ip@/24 dev @iface@
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: addr del is the mirror of addr add
---
q: %show% the routing table
a: ip route
out: .
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: ip route with no filter lists the whole table
---
q: %show% only the default route
a: ip route show default
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: show default filters the route table to just the default route
---
q: {add a default route via @ip@|set @ip@ as the default route|make @ip@ the gateway for the default route}
a: ip route add default via @ip@
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: route add default via sets where traffic with no other match goes
---
q: {delete the default route|remove the default route|take the default route out}
a: ip route del default
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: route del default is the mirror of add
---
q: %show% the arp neighbour table using ip
a: ip neigh
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: ip neigh is ip's own view of the arp table
---
q: %show% just the interface names, one per line
a: ip -o link | cut -d: -f2
out: .
ref: ip
teach: ip
hint: -o lines them up so cut can grab just the name field
---
q: %show% interfaces the old way
a: ifconfig
ref: ifconfig
teach: ifconfig
hint: ifconfig with no arguments lists every interface, the pre-ip tool
---
q: %show% @iface@ specifically, the old way
a: ifconfig @iface@
out: .
ref: ifconfig
teach: ifconfig
hint: name one interface and ifconfig shows only it
---
q: {set @iface@ up with an address, the old way|bring @iface@ up with an address, the old way|the old-way command to set @iface@'s address and bring it up}
a: ifconfig @iface@ @ip@ up
ref: ifconfig
teach: ifconfig
hint: ifconfig combines address and up into one command, unlike ip's two steps
---
q: %show% the routing table the old way
a: route
out: .
ref: route
teach: route
hint: route with no arguments is ifconfig's routing counterpart
---
q: %show% routes without resolving names to make it fast
a: route -n
out: .
ref: route
teach: route -n
hint: -n skips dns lookups on every address in the table
---
q: %show% route table entries with extended fields
a: route -e
out: .
ref: route
teach: route -e
hint: -e adds the extra fields netstat-style output shows
---
q: %show% routes for a named address family
a: route -A inet
out: .
ref: route
teach: route -A
hint: -A takes the address family name, inet for ipv4
---
q: %show% the arp cache
a: arp
out: .
ref: arp
teach: arp
hint: arp with no arguments lists the whole cache
---
q: %show% the arp cache numerically, without resolving names
a: arp -n
out: .
ref: arp
teach: arp -n
hint: -n skips resolving hostnames for each entry
---
q: %show% all arp entries including incomplete ones
a: arp -a
out: .
ref: arp
teach: arp -a
hint: -a includes entries still waiting to resolve
---
q: {delete the arp entry for @ip@|remove the arp entry for @ip@|drop @ip@ from the arp cache}
a: arp -d @ip@
ref: arp
teach: arp -d
hint: -d removes one entry from the cache
---
q: {add a permanent arp entry|pin a permanent arp entry|add a static entry to the arp cache}
a: arp -s @ip@ 00:11:22:33:44:55
ref: arp
teach: arp -s
hint: -s pins an ip to a mac address by hand
---
q: %show% arp entries for one interface only
a: arp -i @iface@
out: .
ref: arp
teach: arp -i
hint: -i filters the cache down to one interface
---
q: %show% the arp cache verbosely
a: arp -v
out: .
ref: arp
teach: arp -v
hint: -v adds detail arp normally leaves out
---
q: %show% arp entries of a given hardware type
a: arp -H ether
out: .
ref: arp
teach: arp -H
hint: -H filters by hardware type, ether being the common case
---
q: {use a named address family for arp|tell arp which address family to use|restrict arp to a named address family}
a: arp -A inet
out: .
ref: arp
teach: arp -A
hint: -A picks the address family, same idea as route -A
---
q: {publish an arp entry on behalf of another host|answer arp requests for another host's address|publish a proxy arp entry for another host}
a: arp -D @iface@ -s @ip@
ref: arp
teach: arp -D
hint: -D answers arp requests for an address on another host's behalf
---
q: {is anyone already using the address @ip@ -- ask at the link layer|check at the link layer whether @ip@ is already in use|find out, below ip, whether @ip@ already has an owner}
a: arping -c 3 @ip@
ref: arping
teach: arping -c
hint: arp works below ip, so this finds hosts that will not answer a ping
---
q: {send the arp probe out of a specific interface|send the arp probe through a chosen interface|pick which interface the arp probe goes out of}
a: arping -I @iface@ -c 3 @ip@
ref: arping
teach: arping -I
hint: -I picks which interface the probe goes out of
---
q: {arping quietly, reporting only the exit status|run arping silently, leaving only the exit status to check|arping with no output, just the exit status}
a: arping -q -c 1 @ip@
ref: arping
teach: arping -q
hint: -q silences output, leaving just the exit status to check
---
q: {send a duplicate-address-detection probe|probe for whether an address is a duplicate|send a duplicate-address-detection check}
a: arping -D -c 1 @ip@
ref: arping
teach: arping -D
hint: -D checks whether an address is already claimed before you use it
---
q: {send an unsolicited arp announcement of your own address|announce your own address unsolicited|broadcast an unsolicited arp announcement of your address}
a: arping -U -c 1 @ip@
ref: arping
teach: arping -U
hint: -U announces your address without being asked
---
q: {arping using a chosen source address|arping with a source address you choose|set the source address arping probes from}
a: arping -s @ip2@ -c 1 @ip@
ref: arping
teach: arping -s
hint: -s sets the source address the probe claims to be from
---
q: {stop arping after 5 seconds whatever happens|cap arping at 5 seconds no matter what|make arping give up after 5 seconds}
a: arping -w 5 @ip@
ref: arping
teach: arping -w
hint: -w caps the total time arping will run
---
q: {keep arping until a reply arrives, then stop|arping until the first reply, then stop|stop arping as soon as a reply comes back}
a: arping -f @ip@
ref: arping
teach: arping -f
hint: -f stops at the first reply instead of sending a fixed count
---
q: {send broadcast arp probes|send the arp probes to the broadcast address|broadcast the arp probes instead of unicasting them}
a: arping -b -c 1 @ip@
ref: arping
teach: arping -b
hint: -b sends to the broadcast address instead of a unicast one
---
q: {arping in a mode that does not require a source address|arping without claiming any source address|run arping so it needs no source address}
a: arping -A -c 1 @ip@
ref: arping
teach: arping -A
hint: -A lets arping run without claiming a source address
---
q: {work out the network settings for the address @ip@/24|derive the network settings for @ip@/24|break down @ip@/24 into its network settings}
a: ipcalc @ip@/24
cmp: ref
ref: ipcalc
teach: ipcalc
hint: ipcalc with no flags prints the whole network breakdown
---
q: %show% just the broadcast address for @ip@/24
a: ipcalc -b @ip@/24
cmp: ref
ref: ipcalc
teach: ipcalc -b
hint: -b isolates just the broadcast address
---
q: %show% just the network address for @ip@/24
a: ipcalc -n @ip@/24
cmp: ref
ref: ipcalc
teach: ipcalc -n
hint: -n isolates just the network address
---
q: %show% just the netmask for a /24
a: ipcalc -m @ip@/24
cmp: ref
ref: ipcalc
teach: ipcalc -m
hint: -m isolates just the netmask
---
q: %show% the prefix length for a given netmask
a: ipcalc -p @ip@ 255.255.255.0
cmp: ref
ref: ipcalc
teach: ipcalc -p
hint: -p converts a dotted netmask into its prefix length
---
q: {get ipcalc output without any resolved hostnames, for a script|run ipcalc without resolved hostnames, for scripting|strip ipcalc's output down to script-friendly form, no hostnames}
a: ipcalc -s @ip@/24
cmp: ref
ref: ipcalc
teach: ipcalc -s
hint: -s silences ipcalc's extra explanatory output
